COMMON PITFALLS
- Solely analyzing how a leader keeps themself in power, without explaining how their leadership achieves some larger purpose.
- Because leadership consists of many possible qualities and behaviors, a danger is to describe many of those superficially without going into depth about any of them.
- Tagging too many potentially complementary HCs that you haven’t applied particularly well, perhaps given the constraints of the assignment.
- Failing to back up interpretations with credible evidence, taking too much for granted.
- Arguing against forms of ethically bad leadership without evaluating the effectiveness of that leadership style.

APPLYING THIS HC AT MINERVA
- Sociology (understanding leadership interactions with groups): A sociologist will look at leadership and focus on the characteristics and dynamics of the group being led (that also might have chosen the leader) and how the leader can influence this group. For example, to understand Hitler’s leadership as a sociologist, you’d want a solidly informed grasp of various parts of German society at the time and how it was changing.
- History and Politics (analyzing examples of leaders and their choices): In many analyses of historical or current political events, we consider the influence of leaders. When studying these figures, try to focus on understanding the structures in which they operate and their interactions with their followers. Use various sources – from biographies and first-hand accounts to contextual sources like newspaper articles – to inform your analysis.
- Business: Some business courses specifically focus on how to effectively lead a business. We celebrate successful leaders, and leadership is a whole area of expertise within various fields of business, whether it is about creating a brand, managing the internal structure of a business, or dealing with stakeholders. Make sure to understand that different types of companies will require different types of leadership. Managing a big bank is very different from creating a startup that comes up with the most innovative solutions and adapts to rapidly changing business environments.
- Group Work (throughout Minerva): You might use #leadprinciples when deciding who should lead a group project when leading the group yourself or just to understand the dynamics in a group. When working in a group, leadership often emerges informally when people look to a particular person for guidance, whether because of their social confidence or their assumed expertise on the project they are working on.  For example, when applying #leadprinciples, you could explain how you observed the gaps in leadership throughout the project and what you did to improve them.

APPLYING THIS HC OUTSIDE MINERVA
- As a leader: You might end up in a manager or leadership role within your career at one point, but you are likely also applying leadership skills when interacting in spaces without a clear hierarchy. You don’t necessarily need a formal leadership position. You can make a change by setting an example or coming up with transformational ideas that strike a chord with those around you!
- Navigating different types of superiors: Whether in an institution like a university or when working a job, you must report to and cooperate with different leaders. Some will be good, some not so much, but understanding their leadership style and qualities will help you encourage good leadership in those above you and give effective feedback on whatever is potentially problematic about a leadership style. Even if some leadership styles annoy you, it can help to see the bigger picture and consider why a particular leader might be acting the way they do.
- Navigating different leadership structures: Sometimes, something in the structure of the organizations we work in stimulates certain kinds of leadership. Suppose an organization has to report on some sort of goals. In that case, it can pressure a leader to focus their leadership on those goals rather than on other purposes like creating group identity or shared learning. Consider addressing the factors that promote or discourage certain types of leadership.
- Voting for or running for leadership positions: Understanding #leadprinciples helps us weigh alternative options for group leadership in political contexts, empowering us to understand their goals and strategies better. As members of political groups, we can also use #leadprinciples ourselves when advocating for policies or political platforms to help us focus our efforts on group-informed goals.

